# AETHER — Product Vision & Strategy

> Working name: **AETHER** (reused from the original bot's dashboard layer, kept as the platform brand for continuity — the in-app live-monitoring screen is literally called "Aether View" as a callback).

---

## 1. One-line pitch

**AETHER is a visual, drag-and-drop builder for algorithmic trading agents** — instead of coding a trading bot from scratch, users assemble one from pre-built, toggleable components (data sources, risk gates, execution logic, self-learning modules), backtest it instantly, and pay only for the layers and compute they actually use.

Think **n8n / Zapier**, but every node is a piece of a trading agent instead of a workflow automation step.

---

## 2. The problem

Building a serious trading agent today requires:
- Fluency in Python/ML to wire together data feeds, models, and execution logic
- Weeks of infrastructure work before you can test a single idea
- No easy way to isolate *which* component of your strategy is actually adding value
- No easy way to share, sell, or fork a working strategy configuration

Most people who have a *trading idea* (e.g. "I think news sentiment + a tighter stop-loss would help") have no fast way to test that idea without building an entire pipeline first.

## 3. The insight

A trading bot is really a **graph of interchangeable components** grouped into layers (data → features → decision models → risk gates → execution → learning → memory). If you expose that graph visually and make every node swappable, you turn "building a trading bot" into "assembling one," the same way no-code tools turned "building a workflow integration" into a drag-and-drop exercise.

## 4. Who this is for

| Segment | What they want |
|---|---|
| **Hobbyist/retail builders** | Want to test a strategy idea without writing infra code |
| **Quant-curious developers** | Want to swap in their own models/data sources without rebuilding the scaffolding |
| **Strategy creators** | Want to package and sell/share a working configuration |
| **Learners** | Want to understand *why* a strategy works by seeing which layer contributed what |

## 5. Core value propositions

1. **Composability** — every layer (data, features, models, risk, execution, learning, memory) is a swappable, toggleable node.
2. **Instant feedback** — backtest results update as you edit the graph; you *see* what each component is worth.
3. **Pay for what you use** — free tier for basic composition and paper testing; pay-as-you-go or subscription to unlock advanced layers (RL, ensemble models, live self-learning) — powered by Razorpay.
4. **A marketplace, not a black box** — users can publish, fork, and (optionally) sell working configurations, with transparent backtest stats attached.
5. **Honest metrics, not vibes** — every backtest reports win rate, drawdown, false-positive-style costs, and what the self-learning loop actually learned — no cherry-picked "look, it's up 40%" screenshots.

## 6. What AETHER is explicitly *not*

- **Not** a signals service telling people what to buy — it's a *tool*, the strategy is theirs.
- **Not** connected to real funds by default — the default and demo mode is **paper trading only**. Live execution with real capital is a clearly gated, opt-in, higher-tier capability with its own risk disclosures — this matters both ethically and because of how India's regulatory environment treats crypto-adjacent products.
- **Not** investment advice, anywhere in the product copy.

## 7. Competitive framing (for context, not for the pitch deck)

- **n8n / Zapier / Make** — proved the "visual node graph for logic" pattern works at scale, but for generic workflows, not decision-making agents with feedback loops.
- **TradingView Pine Script / QuantConnect** — powerful but code-first; steep learning curve, no visual composability.
- **3Commas / Pionex** — pre-built bot templates, but closed-box; you tweak parameters, not architecture.
- **AETHER's gap to fill**: visual composability *of the architecture itself*, not just parameters — plus a self-learning/memory layer that most retail tools don't expose at all.

## 8. Monetization philosophy

Money should flow at the point where a user gets **real incremental value**, not before:
- Assembling and paper-backtesting a *basic* bot (a handful of layers) is free — this is the "aha" moment and must never be paywalled, or nobody discovers the value.
- Depth (ensembles, RL, multi-agent debate, always-on self-learning, higher-frequency live paper trading, real-fund execution) is where the paywall lives.
- A **marketplace revenue share** lets strategy creators earn from presets others fork/subscribe to — Razorpay Route can split payments between platform and creator automatically.

Full mechanics are in `06_Monetization_and_Razorpay_Integration.md`.

## 9. Why this fits an Open Innovation track

It doesn't try to force a merchant-commerce or fraud-detection narrative onto a trading bot (that would be dishonest scoping). Instead it's honestly framed as: **a no-code platform with a real, Razorpay-powered subscription and marketplace payment business model** — which is a legitimate product a payments company would recognize as relevant to their own ecosystem, without pretending the underlying tool is something it isn't.

## 10. Success criteria for a hackathon demo

A judge should be able to watch, in under 5 minutes:
1. A bot assembled from scratch by dragging 3–4 nodes and connecting them
2. A backtest run live on screen with real (or realistic historical) data, showing a results dashboard
3. A locked/premium layer being unlocked via an actual Razorpay checkout, and the graph changing as a result
4. The bot saved as a preset and shared/forked by a "second user" (can be a second browser tab/demo account)

See `07_MVP_Scope_and_Buildathon_Demo_Plan.md` for exactly how to scope this in a week.
